1
00:00:01,399 --> 00:00:03,182
Last night, sixteen monkeys found the

2
00:00:03,142 --> 00:00:04,830
exact sum of their private numbers.

3
00:00:04,790 --> 00:00:06,808
Each one is a language model: Qwen3.8-27B.

4
00:00:06,918 --> 00:00:08,263
They did it by flooding.

5
00:00:08,223 --> 00:00:11,035
Every monkey collected every other monkey's number,

6
00:00:10,995 --> 00:00:11,796
two to a port.

7
00:00:11,907 --> 00:00:13,204
So we made the grid bigger.

8
00:00:13,564 --> 00:00:14,580
One hundred monkeys.

9
00:00:14,540 --> 00:00:16,969
Sixty ticks later, not one had the sum: 0 of 100.

10
00:00:17,079 --> 00:00:19,270
Each held about 90 of the 100 numbers.

11
00:00:19,230 --> 00:00:21,702
Several announced the network was complete.

12
00:00:21,812 --> 00:00:23,215
It wasn't the monkeys.

13
00:00:23,175 --> 00:00:25,320
A port carries two numbers a tick.

14
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:28,231
Even perfect agents need 50 ticks to flood 100.

15
00:00:28,341 --> 00:00:29,468
And every monkey's notes grow with the crowd:

16
00:00:29,428 --> 00:00:30,435
prompts went from 1,300 tokens to 5,600.

17
00:00:30,795 --> 00:00:32,161
The fix is from 1982.

18
00:00:32,121 --> 00:00:34,435
Don't make everyone know everything.

19
00:00:34,395 --> 00:00:35,889
Add it up along a tree.

20
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:37,380
One monkey starts a wave.

21
00:00:37,340 --> 00:00:40,167
Whoever it reaches takes that neighbour as a parent,

22
00:00:40,127 --> 00:00:41,400
and passes the wave on.

23
00:00:41,511 --> 00:00:43,945
When everyone below a monkey has answered,

24
00:00:43,905 --> 00:00:45,313
it sends up one message:

25
00:00:45,273 --> 00:00:46,396
a sum, and a count.

26
00:00:46,356 --> 00:00:47,821
The host does the adding.

27
00:00:47,932 --> 00:00:50,673
The root gets the total, and knows it's complete.

28
00:00:50,633 --> 00:00:52,381
Then the answer runs back down.

29
00:00:52,741 --> 00:00:55,808
Same hundred monkeys. Same model. Tick 23:

30
00:00:55,768 --> 00:00:59,268
the first exact total. Tick 33: all one hundred.

31
00:00:59,378 --> 00:01:01,987
49,689. No wrong answers.

32
00:01:01,947 --> 00:01:06,509
A perfect tree takes 32 ticks. They took 33.

33
00:01:06,618 --> 00:01:08,582
Each tick ran in half the time (202 s against 375),

34
00:01:08,542 --> 00:01:09,677
and the prompts stayed small.

35
00:01:10,036 --> 00:01:11,430
A tree has one weakness.

36
00:01:11,390 --> 00:01:14,477
One slip, and a whole branch vanishes without a sound.

37
00:01:14,587 --> 00:01:16,770
It happened, in a Claude Haiku run.

38
00:01:16,730 --> 00:01:18,913
One monkey waved at its own parent,

39
00:01:18,873 --> 00:01:21,851
and 11 dropped out of the count: 44,294 over 89.

40
00:01:21,962 --> 00:01:23,764
So grow two trees from two roots,

41
00:01:23,724 --> 00:01:26,006
and trust the answer only when they agree.

42
00:01:25,966 --> 00:01:27,395
The second tree caught it.

43
00:01:27,504 --> 00:01:29,801
Now the host refuses that write. The rerun:

44
00:01:29,761 --> 00:01:31,481
100 of 100, both trees agreeing.

45
00:01:31,841 --> 00:01:33,687
It isn't one model's trick.

46
00:01:33,647 --> 00:01:36,028
A swarm of 112 Claude Haiku agents,

47
00:01:35,988 --> 00:01:38,769
across seven arrays joined by slow seams:

48
00:01:38,729 --> 00:01:41,311
112 of 112 exact, for half the tokens.

49
00:01:41,671 --> 00:01:44,714
Then the big one. 256 monkeys on a tesseract:

50
00:01:44,674 --> 00:01:47,717
four dimensions, one ring through every port.

51
00:01:47,828 --> 00:01:51,298
No tree here. Each monkey sends one entry around its ring,

52
00:01:51,258 --> 00:01:53,546
and everyone it passes adds their own.

53
00:01:53,506 --> 00:01:55,617
Four rings, and you hold the total.

54
00:01:55,725 --> 00:01:58,098
Perfect agents need 17 ticks.

55
00:01:58,058 --> 00:02:00,029
Flooding would need 128.

56
00:02:00,138 --> 00:02:02,785
It broke. One monkey's number was counted twice,

57
00:02:02,745 --> 00:02:05,554
and every monkey after it passed the mistake along.

58
00:02:05,664 --> 00:02:10,057
Claude Haiku: 186 of 256 exact, 70 confidently wrong.

59
00:02:10,017 --> 00:02:13,426
And Qwen3.8-27B failed with only sixteen.

60
00:02:13,536 --> 00:02:15,956
A ring has no second tree to catch a slip.

61
00:02:15,916 --> 00:02:19,016
So next, the host checks every write. That's tomorrow.

62
00:02:19,478 --> 00:02:21,152
Nobody has to know everything.

63
00:02:21,112 --> 00:02:22,569
But somebody has to check.

64
00:02:22,830 --> 00:02:24,157
Pass it up, little monkeys.

65
00:02:24,117 --> 00:02:25,063
We keep the ledger.

